### Drill 2: Control Plane Static Pod Recovery (`02-static-pod-recovery/`)

Control plane components (`etcd`, `kube-apiserver`, `kube-controller-manager`, and `kube-scheduler`) are managed
directly by the local `kubelet` daemon as **Static Pods** defined in `/etc/kubernetes/manifests/`.

#### Exam Symptom: Applications Stuck in Pending

When the scheduler fails, existing pods continue running, but all newly created Deployments or Pods remain stuck in
`Pending` indefinitely with zero scheduling events recorded in `kubectl describe pod`.

#### Step 1: Simulate Scheduler Manifest Corruption & Deploy Canary

Execute the failure injector script (defaults to Scenario 1: broken kubeconfig path):

```bash
cd {{ cka_lab_cluster_troubleshooting_dir }}/02-static-pod-recovery

# Scenario 1 (Default): Broken kubeconfig path
sudo ./break-scheduler.sh

# Or test alternative scenarios:
# sudo ./break-scheduler.sh --scenario=mount   (Corrupted hostPath mount)
# sudo ./break-scheduler.sh --scenario=syntax  (Invalid YAML syntax; mirror pod disappears)
```

The script automatically applies a canary probe deployment (`cka-scheduler-probe`) in the `default` namespace.

#### Step 2: Observe the Symptom (Pending Pod with Zero Events)

```bash
# Check the canary pod status
kubectl get pods -n default -l app=scheduler-probe

# Describe pod: notice the Events section has ZERO scheduling entries
kubectl describe pod -n default -l app=scheduler-probe
```

#### Step 3: Investigate Control Plane Pod Status

Check the health of core components in `kube-system`:

```bash
kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l component=kube-scheduler
```

Notice the status shows `CrashLoopBackOff` or `Error`. (If testing Scenario 3 with syntax corruption, notice the
static pod mirror pod completely disappears from the listing).

#### Step 4: Extract Autopsy Logs

```bash
# When mirror pod is present, extract logs via kubectl:
kubectl logs -n kube-system -l component=kube-scheduler --tail=50

# When component is failing or API server is unreachable, inspect via crictl:
sudo crictl ps -a | grep -i scheduler
sudo crictl logs <container-id>

# When mirror pod is absent (syntax error), inspect kubelet systemd journal:
sudo journalctl -u kubelet -e --no-pager | grep -i scheduler
```

> [!TIP]
> **What if `kube-apiserver` is down?**
> If the API server itself is broken, `kubectl` commands fail with `The connection to the server was refused`. In this
> scenario, you must inspect the container runtime directly on the host using `crictl`:
>
> ```bash
> sudo crictl ps -a | grep -E "apiserver|scheduler"
> sudo crictl logs <container-id>
> ```

#### Step 5: Fix the Static Pod Manifest

Static pod manifests are located at `/etc/kubernetes/manifests/`. Edit the corrupted manifest:

```bash
# Locate and edit the scheduler manifest
sudo vim /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-scheduler.yaml

# Alternatively, execute the automated restoration script
sudo ./fix-scheduler.sh
```

Upon saving the file, the local `kubelet` detects the change via inotify file watchers and automatically recreates the
static pod. `fix-scheduler.sh` verifies that the scheduler is `Running`, confirms the canary workload schedules,
and cleans up the probe deployment.

#### Step 6: Verify Recovery or Run Automated Self-Test

```bash
# Verify scheduler pod is healthy
kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l component=kube-scheduler

# Run full automated drill self-test:
sudo ./test-static-pod-recovery.sh
```

